## Explainability

SHAP was used to analyze the LightGBM model.

The most influential features included:

- C5
- TransactionAmt
- D3
- C14
- card6

Because these features are anonymized, their names cannot directly be
translated into human-readable fraud reasons.

For the final ORION³ system, interpretable behavioral features will be
generated by the scam simulator instead.

## Threshold Analysis

At approximately 1% false-positive rate:

- Threshold: **0.2953**
- False-positive rate: **~1.00%**
- Recall: **43.87%**

This illustrates the trade-off between detecting fraud and generating
false alarms.

## Cost Analysis

An illustrative cost model was used:

- Missed fraud cost = transaction amount
- False-positive cost = fixed cost

On the tuning/validation set:

| Strategy | Total Loss | Loss Saved |
|---|---:|---:|
| Flag nothing | 609,934.31 | 0% |
| Flag everything | 1,140,440.00 | -86.98% |
| Threshold 0.5 | 443,677.50 | 27.26% |
| Optimized threshold ≈ 0.06 | 271,528.75 | **55.48%** |

The cost values are illustrative and depend on the assumed
false-positive cost.

### Cost Sensitivity

| False-positive cost | Best threshold |
|---:|---:|
| 5 | 0.0311 |
| 10 | 0.0612 |
| 50 | 0.1966 |
| 100 | 0.4624 |

This demonstrates that the optimal operating threshold changes
substantially depending on the business cost assigned to false alarms.

